\details{
\subsection{Principle}{

A \code{contMap} stores the trait history as a series of short segments along each branch,
with ancestral node values typically estimated from a maximum likelihood reconstruction under a Brownian motion (BM) model
and linearly interpolated along the branches.

During discretization, each segment is assigned to the bin its value falls in, and consecutive segments of the same bin are merged.
A value equal to a cut point belongs to the bin above it. The result is a \code{simmap} (as in \code{\link[phytools:make.simmap]{phytools::make.simmap()}}) where the states are the bins.
}

\subsection{Cutting the trait values}{

Provide exactly one of the three arguments:
\itemize{
\item \code{breaks}: the cut points are chosen by the user.
\item \code{n_bins}: uses bins of equal width over the range of the tip values.
\item \code{probs}: uses bins defined by quantiles of the tip values.
}

\code{n_bins} and \code{probs} are computed on the tip values (averaged across the \code{contMap}s of a list), which are the observed data.
The values of the internal branches are reconstructed and are expected to shrink towards the intermediate values under a BM process
(or towards one specific direction if a directional model was used to fit the \code{contMap}s), because ancestral values are averaged over their descendants.
As a consequence, bins defined from the tips will contain a smaller share of the internal branches for the extreme states than for the intermediate states.

\code{breaks} must be interior cut points: they must lie within the range of the trait values, i.e.,
between the minimum and the maximum of the values of all the \code{contMap}s. A cut point beyond this range is an error.
A cut point equal to the minimum or the maximum is ignored with a warning, because it would define an empty bin.

The cut points must be in increasing order and different values. This is checked for the three arguments, including the cut points derived from \code{n_bins} and \code{probs}
(e.g., many tips sharing the same value can give identical quantiles). Unsorted cut points are sorted, and duplicated values are removed, with an informative warning.

The number of \code{states} must be equal to the number of bins (the number of cut points + 1, after the cut points equal to the boundaries were ignored).
Removing cut points reduces the number of bins, so \code{states} must match the number of bins left.
}

\subsection{Short segments}{

When using \code{contMap}s derived from continuous stochastic mapping processes such as the one featured in the \code{contsimmap} R package,
trait values can wander around a cut point: the trait evolutionary history can crosses the threshold several times over short distances,
resulting in tiny segments alternating states.
Segments shorter than \code{min_segment_proportion} times the depth of the tree are merged, from the shortest to the longest, into their longest neighbour on the same branch.
A branch shorter than the minimum length gets a single state: the one with the longest total duration, or the state of the tip for a terminal branch.
On a terminal branch, the segment at the tip is never merged away to ensure tip states matched with observed tip data.
Setting \code{min_segment_proportion = 0} keeps all the crossings.

This is not an issue for \code{contMap} featuring linear interpolation of ancestral trait values as in \code{\link[phytools:contMap]{phytools::contMap()}},
since trait values can only vary monotonously along branches (i.e., decrease, increase, or remain stable, but not a combination of two),
thus cannot cross each cut point multiple times.
}

\subsection{Cautions for interpretation}{
\itemize{
\item The resulting \code{simmap}s is/are a discretization of the reconstructed trait to constrain the grafting of missing taxa.
It is not a stochastic map of a discrete trait: the number of transitions depends on the resolution of the \code{contMap} and on \code{min_segment_proportion}
and should not be interpreted as a rate of evolution of discrete states.
\item Nodes with reconstructed values close to a cut point have uncertain states. Providing a list of \code{contMap}s
covering the uncertainty (see below) is the way to account for it, as each grafted phylogeny can then use a different \code{simmap}.
\item As for the use of \code{simmap} derived from discrete states, constraining the grafting by a trait that will then be analyzed
(e.g., the association of the trait with diversification) biases the results towards the constraint. Interpret such analyses accordingly.
}
}

\subsection{Accounting for uncertainty in trait estimates: continuous stochastic mapping}{

A single \code{contMap} is a point estimate. To cover the uncertainty of the reconstruction, provide a list of \code{contMap}s, each one built from one possible history of the trait.
For example, the \code{contsimmap} R package samples histories of a continuous trait on a phylogeny, and the function \code{\link[deepSTRAPP:convert_contsimmap_to_contMaps]{deepSTRAPP::convert_contsimmap_to_contMaps()}}
converts them into a list of \code{contMap}s (see Examples). The output of this function is then a \code{multiSimmap}, that can be used in the grafting functions like any \code{multiSimmap},
to account for uncertainty in the ancestral trait reconstructions.
}
